This job advertisement is to fill an existing vacancy in the Administrative Professional (Employee Group)
 About the Role : 
 The Laboratory Supervisor - Engineering is responsible for designing, implementing, coordinating, testing, and maintaining hands-on engineering laboratory activities, with particular emphasis on robotics, automation and electrical systems. The role will provide support for various lab teaching facilities, including robotics, circuits and instrumentation, programmable logic controllers (PLCs), industrial control panels, sensors and actuators, and selected mechanical and structural testing equipment. The incumbent is expected to provide safe, high-quality laboratory demonstrations and supervise students and teaching assistants (TAs) during laboratory activities. 
 The Laboratory Supervisor will:  
 Work with course instructors and faculty to design, develop and prepare lab demonstrations that are current and relevant to the course curricula; 
 Work closely with faculty, students, and other staff to support the department's educational goals; 
 Develop learning objectives, lab manuals, and technical presentation materials for laboratory demonstrations, commensurate with current technology and knowledge base; 
 Perform demonstrations and experiments, directing students through laboratory activities by explaining, observing, correcting, and guiding throughout, ensuring quality and consistency in laboratory delivery; 
 Maintain detailed records of experiments, equipment logs, calibration logs, and inventory reports; 
 Manage laboratory resources and inspect laboratory equipment for operational problems; 
 Maintain and record student grades related to laboratory activities and assessments in the current electronic format (Brightspace); 
 Advise and resolve students on issues and concerns, remaining current on undergraduate policy and regulations, the Faculty Handbook, and Office of Registrar policy and procedures; 
 Facilitate the recruitment and selection of Teaching Assistants (TAs) and Student positions; and 
 Schedule and direct the work of TAs (i.e., coordinate and assign teaching duties within a lab section, train, supervise, provide direction, etc.). 
 Key Skills and Experience: 
 BSc or MSc degree in Robotics, Electrical, Electronics, or Mechatronics Engineering (or a closely related engineering discipline), and/or hands-on experience as an engineering technician, technologist, or laboratory supervisor; 
 Demonstrated experience with robotic systems and platforms (e.g., industrial robot arms, collaborative robots, mobile/autonomous robots), including robot kinematics, programming, sensor integration, and end-effector interfacing; 
 Demonstrated experience in robotic, electrical, electronic, mechatronic, and automation setups, including control panels, PLC-based systems, motor drives, sensors, actuators, and electromechanical equipment etc. and experience in developing, preparing, and delivering practical laboratory instruction in the above areas; 
 Demonstrated knowledge and training in electrical safety, machine guarding, lockout/tagout procedures, safe use of laboratory and machine-shop equipment, and safe operation of industrial automation systems; 
 Commitment to laboratory safety standards and procedures; 
 Excellent understanding of course materials with related experience teaching at the University level, including familiarity with the administrative program requirements; 
 Demonstrated ability to effectively explain, observe, correct, and guide students in a laboratory environment; 
 Proficiency in standard computer programs and software, including Microsoft Office, Gradebook, and Brightspace (or equivalent); 
 Exceptional interpersonal skills including discretion, and confidentiality;  
 Ability to develop laboratory exercises that complement course content, utilizing current technology, and aligning with student knowledge base; 
 Strong multi-tasking, prioritization, and organizational skills, with the ability to adhere to strict deadlines and keep accurate records;  
 Ability to solve problems in ad-hoc situations in a logical and effective manner. 
